-- title:
--   A bound on the number of reference cones depending only on the aperture
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Then there is a natural number $L$, depending only on $E$ and $\vartheta$, such that for **every** $\vartheta$-bounded configuration $\Gamma$ on $E$ — that is, every assignment $x \mapsto \Gamma(x)$ of a double cone with unit axis and apex angle at least $\vartheta$ — there exists a configuration $\Gamma'$ with
--
--   $$\text{(i) } \#\{\Gamma'(x) : x \in E\} \le L; \qquad
--   \text{(ii) } \Gamma'(x) \subseteq \Gamma(x) \text{ for all } x; \qquad
--   \text{(iii) } \Gamma'(x).\mathrm{apex} = \vartheta/3 \text{ for all } x; \qquad
--   \text{(iv) } \Gamma' \text{ is } (\vartheta/3)\text{-bounded}.$$
--
--   This is the reduction to finitely many reference cones in the form in which the bound is *uniform*: the number $L$ of cone types is quantified before the configuration, so all later constants built from a type count depend only on the dimension and on $\vartheta$, never on the particular configuration. That uniformity is what allows the constants in the chaining and counting arguments to be configuration-independent.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The cardinality bound is stated with the extended natural number cardinality of the range, $\mathrm{encard} \le L$ in $\mathbb{N}^\infty$, which simultaneously asserts finiteness and the bound.
